    {
      "title": "Accessibility",
      "nodeId": "1330:933",
      "sections": [
        {
          "level": 3,
          "paragraphs": [
            "A badge is a small element that communicates a status, a count, a label or a notification on another element."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "What the design system provides",
          "level": 3,
          "paragraphs": [
            "•   Static badges have no focusable states, so they never add stray tab stops.",
            "•   Each status color maps to one meaning.",
            "•   Text badges carry their meaning in the label, not in color alone."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Development considerations",
          "level": 3,
          "paragraphs": [
            "•   A static badge is not interactive — don't give it a button role.",
            "•   Dot badges have no text: expose the status they signal on the element they annotate, so meaning never relies on color alone.",
            "•   The dismiss control needs its own accessible name and must be separately focusable.",
            "•   A clickable badge needs a visible focus ring."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Keyboard interactions",
          "level": 3,
          "paragraphs": [
            "Key",
            "Action",
            "Tab",
            "Moves focus to the dismiss control (dismissible badges only)",
            "Shift",
            "+",
            "Tab",
            "Moves focus to the previous focusable element"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Design recommendations",
          "level": 3,
          "paragraphs": [
            "Automated contrast check — WCAG 2.x relative luminance (sRGB), for every theme combination. Label vs. badge fill (or page surface for Outlined): 4.5:1. Dot vs. page surface: 3:1 (non-text). Note: project-a / light is measured against the current surface/bg/default, which resolves to blue because of the gray/50 token."
          ]
